WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:17.600
MUSIC

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.760
There are more kinds of insects in the world

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:30.960
than all other kinds of animals put together.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:35.760
But because they're so small, we rarely see how extraordinary they are.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.480
Occasionally, they assemble in astonishing numbers.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:57.040
MUSIC

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:01.360
A billion monarch butterflies hibernate in the forests of Mexico.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:10.240
MUSIC

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:13.840
They can transform their bodies to meet every purpose.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.000
Eyes grow on stalks.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:18.640
Bodies become chemical guns.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:23.200
MUSIC

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:26.240
To discover how and why they do such things,

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:28.480
you have to enter their world.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:33.600
MUSIC

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:40.960
WIND BLOWS

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:47.360
WIND BLOWS

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:54.800
WIND BLOWS

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:57.440
Patagonia, Chile.

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:03.840
Home to one of the most extraordinary of beetles.

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:06.400
There are many kinds of beetles in the world,

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:10.800
but this one has some of the biggest jaws of all.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:12.720
He is Darwin's beetle.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:22.200
He's on his way to the forest to look for a mate.

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:26.800
Whether he gets one or not will depend on his strength

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:28.720
and on the size of his jaws.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.240
BIRDS CHIRP

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:40.320
BIRDS CHIRP

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:42.240
He starts his search.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:48.560
A female is likely to be on a tree trunk.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:54.320
But trees in this part of the world are very tall.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:58.800
His search could be a long one.

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:11.120
Unfortunately for him, she is 25 metres above him, near the top.

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:14.800
She has more normal-sized jaws,

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.920
but then she only needs them for feeding.

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:22.320
But he needs immense jaws for fighting.

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:28.160
Because there are other males around with the same mission.

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:35.200
Shears strength is not enough in these battles.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:38.320
The technique is to reach over your opponent's head

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:42.000
and hook your jaws onto the tree trunk.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:52.800
The

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:59.600
technique is to reach over your opponent's head and hook your jaws under his wing covers.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:04.880
That's why his jaws are so long and have that odd shape.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:08.160
He's got the grip.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:11.040
Now he has to lift.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:20.240
And that needs strength.

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:26.240
Another lift is needed.

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:33.280
And that's that.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:37.760
Beetle armour is strong, so he bounces.

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:40.320
The winner climbs on.

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:44.160
There are more males ready to fight him up here.

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:53.840
He bounces.

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:29.840
And here she is at last.

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:53.200
But she doesn't seem to be in the mood.

00:05:53.200 --> 00:06:05.040
So now he has to use his great jaws as a restraining cage.

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:13.840
Success at last.

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:24.240
But the hurling habit dies hard.

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:31.360
Insects bodies have the capacity to take on an almost infinite variety of shapes.

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:52.000
This is one of the keys to their success.

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:56.800
This male moth doesn't go in search of females.

00:06:56.800 --> 00:07:03.520
He inflates a pair of scented plumes that protrude from the end of his abdomen.

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:13.440
And they come to him.

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:20.080
All insects have the same basic design, six legs and an armour covered body.

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:31.120
But the different parts can become exaggerated to solve particular problems.

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:40.400
This beetle has a giraffe's neck in order to fight.

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:48.080
A praying mantis is disguised as a flower and waits in ambush.

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:54.000
Another is almost invisible among dead leaves.

00:07:54.080 --> 00:08:15.440
Insect armour can carry colours that can camouflage or communicate.

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:19.760
But the armour can also be converted into wings.

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:22.640
And that makes insects a global force,

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:42.960
enabling them to invade almost every habitat on Earth.

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:54.480
In southern France, one insect is starting on the most important day of its life.

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:56.800
Dawn.

00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:02.400
And a newly hatched damselfly.

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:06.400
She needs to mate and lay her eggs before sunset.

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:11.600
Her adult life is so short, she may not get another chance.

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:21.840
Her success will depend on these fragile wings.

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:37.840
She faces many perils.

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:45.520
Few insects can escape from a spider's web.

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:56.080
But her wings are more powerful than they look.

00:09:56.160 --> 00:10:01.280
She wants to mate with the strongest male.

00:10:01.280 --> 00:10:13.360
However, the stream where she might find him is a battleground.

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:18.240
She watches while dark-winged males compete for the best territories,

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:21.600
flashing their wings at each other in threat,

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:30.560
or trying to force their opponents into the water.

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:45.200
Fighting finished, the female uses her wings to signal her presence to the winner.

00:10:45.280 --> 00:10:57.200
She won't be rushed, but if he doesn't grip her with his tail, a rival might steal her.

00:10:57.200 --> 00:11:05.040
As they mate, their linked bodies form the shape of a heart.

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:18.800
But now she has the problem of laying her fertilised eggs, and that brings new hazards.

00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:23.280
Like any athlete, she must warm her muscles for optimum performance

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:29.280
and does so by using her wings as solar heating panels.

00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:52.480
She must fly dangerously low if she's to find the right place for her eggs.

00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:56.640
Only the quickest damselfires escape.

00:12:23.440 --> 00:12:31.600
Safely past the frogs, this female has reached a suitable plant on which to lay her eggs.

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:35.920
But she has to place them under water if they're to survive.

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:39.600
For the first time, her wings seem to be a hindrance,

00:12:39.680 --> 00:12:44.880
but in fact they help her yet again by trapping a silvery layer of air

00:12:44.960 --> 00:12:50.800
which enables her to breathe.

00:12:50.800 --> 00:13:11.120
She cuts into a plant stem and deposits her eggs inside.

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:15.040
Now her wings are going to be truly tested.

00:13:15.040 --> 00:13:24.960
If she can't break through the water surface, she will drown.

00:13:45.040 --> 00:13:54.960
Her day has been a complete success.

00:13:55.040 --> 00:13:58.960
Damselflies seldom venture beyond their home stream,

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:04.960
but some insects fly huge distances.

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:08.960
September, on the shores of Lake Erie in southern Canada,

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:12.960
a monarch butterfly is found.

00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:24.880
A monarch butterfly is fuelling up on nectar.

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:29.680
A chill gust of northerly air.

00:14:29.760 --> 00:14:34.880
It's time to leave.

00:14:34.880 --> 00:14:46.800
The coming winter will be so cold it would kill her.

00:14:46.880 --> 00:14:52.800
She has never flown more than a few hundred metres in her life.

00:14:52.800 --> 00:15:12.720
But now she is heading out over Lake Erie, which is 100 miles across.

00:15:12.800 --> 00:15:18.720
This is just the first leg of one of the world's greatest animal migrations.

00:15:18.720 --> 00:15:22.640
She will continue south, using the sun as a compass,

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:32.640
to cross America, a journey of 2,000 miles.

00:15:32.720 --> 00:15:38.640
Her destination is Mexico and one small and special group of trees.

00:15:38.720 --> 00:15:46.640
No-one knows how she finds them in these great mountain forests.

00:15:46.640 --> 00:15:49.560
She joins other monarch butterflies

00:15:49.640 --> 00:15:57.560
that have travelled here from all over North America.

00:15:57.640 --> 00:16:01.560
Countless butterflies crowd these particular trees,

00:16:01.640 --> 00:16:12.560
hanging from every branch.

00:16:12.560 --> 00:16:16.480
They come here because although winter brings a chill to the air,

00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:21.480
there will not be a lethal freeze of the will in Canada.

00:16:21.560 --> 00:16:31.480
The conditions are perfect for hibernation.

00:16:31.560 --> 00:16:36.480
Hibernating butterflies are vulnerable to predators,

00:16:36.560 --> 00:16:39.480
but monarchs are poisonous.

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:44.400
However, a few birds have learnt to rip out the toxic parts and eat the rest.

00:16:44.480 --> 00:16:53.400
They kill hundreds of thousands of butterflies and dislodge many more.

00:16:53.480 --> 00:16:56.400
Those that fall must get back into the trees

00:16:56.480 --> 00:16:59.400
before nightfall brings another killer.

00:16:59.480 --> 00:17:02.400
They vibrate their wings to warm their flight muscles,

00:17:02.400 --> 00:17:10.320
but it's a race against time.

00:17:10.320 --> 00:17:33.240
Night brings a lethal ground frost.

00:17:40.320 --> 00:18:03.240
Those that do survive sleep safely huddled together in the trees for four months.

00:18:03.320 --> 00:18:09.240
The warmth of spring wakes them from their hibernation.

00:18:09.240 --> 00:18:23.160
Music

00:18:23.240 --> 00:18:34.160
Music

00:19:04.160 --> 00:19:27.040
The majority of the butterflies that flew here from Canada have survived.

00:19:27.040 --> 00:19:36.540
They take their first drink since their arrival last autumn.

00:19:36.540 --> 00:19:53.480
With increasing warmth, more and more butterflies awake.

00:19:53.480 --> 00:19:59.920
Soon they will all disperse northwards and tranquility will return to this forest, until

00:19:59.920 --> 00:20:19.600
the great-grandchildren of these butterflies return to escape the freezing northern winter.

00:20:30.920 --> 00:20:43.840
Some insects, however, have made a speciality of living in the most adverse conditions.

00:20:43.840 --> 00:20:49.480
Mono Lake is known as California's Dead Sea.

00:20:49.480 --> 00:21:01.480
It's twice as salty as the ocean and lethal to almost all life.

00:21:01.480 --> 00:21:07.600
Alkali flies are one of the few creatures able to tolerate these conditions.

00:21:07.600 --> 00:21:34.840
And since they have virtually no competitors, they swarm in their millions.

00:21:34.840 --> 00:21:38.560
Mono lakes are one of the most successful of all insects.

00:21:38.560 --> 00:21:45.480
There are 85,000 different kinds and among them a perfect fly for almost every habitat,

00:21:45.480 --> 00:22:04.080
even this one.

00:22:04.080 --> 00:22:09.920
The alkali fly has special abilities that enable it to live on this lake.

00:22:09.920 --> 00:22:15.840
It can walk underwater to eat algae, fine hairs, trap air bubbles that prevent it from

00:22:15.840 --> 00:22:16.840
drowning.

00:22:16.840 --> 00:22:25.400
But most critically, the fly has a physiology that can neutralize the lethal levels of salt.

00:22:25.400 --> 00:22:33.240
So the alkali fly's dominance of Mono Lake goes unchallenged, except for a few weeks

00:22:33.240 --> 00:22:39.200
of the year.

00:22:39.200 --> 00:22:49.680
100,000 Wilson's Palarope are migrating to South America.

00:22:49.680 --> 00:22:52.880
Mono Lake is their only stopover.

00:22:52.880 --> 00:23:05.360
These alkali flies for them are an invaluable and irresistible feast.

00:23:05.360 --> 00:23:17.560
The fly has no defense except to fly and become lost in a vast swarm.

00:23:17.560 --> 00:23:24.960
It needs considerable skill to pluck an individual fly from the air.

00:23:24.960 --> 00:23:30.920
There are gulls here too.

00:23:30.920 --> 00:23:53.360
Though their fly catching techniques are not so refined.

00:23:53.360 --> 00:24:10.320
In four weeks, the Palaropes double their weight.

00:24:10.320 --> 00:24:21.600
Then they set off again heading south, leaving the gulls behind.

00:24:21.600 --> 00:24:26.000
The size of the fly population has scarcely been affected.

00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:42.160
For them, sheer abundance is defense enough.

00:24:42.160 --> 00:24:49.240
But for some insects, defense is a much more complicated affair.

00:24:49.240 --> 00:24:53.040
An Oogps the beetle in South Africa.

00:24:53.040 --> 00:25:03.920
He hunts ants.

00:25:03.920 --> 00:25:07.240
Eating ants may give him more than just nourishment.

00:25:07.240 --> 00:25:22.440
He may get something else from them that helps him fight his enemies.

00:25:22.440 --> 00:25:28.480
The ants launch a counter attack and nip his ankles.

00:25:28.480 --> 00:25:42.000
But he simply kicks them out of the way.

00:25:42.000 --> 00:25:51.600
The valiant ants drive him off straight into real danger.

00:25:51.600 --> 00:25:58.280
A mongoose.

00:25:58.280 --> 00:26:02.240
It's inquisitive.

00:26:02.240 --> 00:26:05.840
But it's also wary of the Oogps.

00:26:05.840 --> 00:26:09.680
A black and white pattern is a warning signal.

00:26:09.680 --> 00:26:24.820
The beetle takes aim and fires formic acids straight at the mongoose's eyes and mouth.

00:26:24.820 --> 00:26:28.000
The beetle probably collected this acid from the ants.

00:26:28.000 --> 00:26:30.920
It certainly makes the beetle itself very distasteful.

00:26:30.920 --> 00:26:34.880
And that, in turn, makes it worth mimicking.

00:26:34.880 --> 00:26:41.200
This defenseless little lizard carries the beetle's warning pattern.

00:26:41.200 --> 00:26:47.560
It also imitates the way the beetle runs.

00:26:47.560 --> 00:26:52.200
Not particularly well, it's true, but well enough to fool predators into thinking it

00:26:52.200 --> 00:27:06.240
just might be an acid-firing beetle.

00:27:06.240 --> 00:27:12.880
All kinds of insects have developed chemical weapons.

00:27:12.880 --> 00:27:19.340
A pair of Devilrider stick insects.

00:27:19.340 --> 00:27:28.240
They fire bitter-tasting oils, terpenes.

00:27:28.240 --> 00:27:33.200
European wood ants under attack from a hungry crow.

00:27:33.200 --> 00:27:36.800
They fire the sort of acid that gives nettles their sting.

00:27:36.800 --> 00:27:50.640
So this is like one of us falling into a nettle patch.

00:27:50.640 --> 00:27:56.760
But the master of chemical warfare is the Bombardier beetle.

00:27:56.760 --> 00:28:02.640
It can create a chemical reaction within its body so violent that boiling caustic liquid

00:28:02.640 --> 00:28:14.000
explodes out of its abdomen.

00:28:14.000 --> 00:28:19.640
By pulsing the jet 500 times a second, it keeps its rear end just cool enough to prevent

00:28:19.640 --> 00:28:39.640
it being cooked.

00:28:39.640 --> 00:28:46.440
In the woodlands of Minnesota, there is an insect that has another chemical weapon.

00:28:46.440 --> 00:29:02.600
And this one fights as an army and can stand up to almost any predator.

00:29:02.600 --> 00:29:08.560
Bees collect nectar all day and transform it within their stomachs into one of nature's

00:29:08.560 --> 00:29:16.080
richest foods, honey.

00:29:16.080 --> 00:29:20.400
Hundreds of them are bringing their precious loads back to their colony in this hollow

00:29:20.400 --> 00:29:32.200
log.

00:29:32.200 --> 00:29:38.120
The honey in their combs provides food for their young and for the whole colony if the

00:29:38.120 --> 00:29:54.520
weather turns cold or there's a drought.

00:29:54.520 --> 00:30:01.560
But so much food attracts thieves.

00:30:01.560 --> 00:30:08.200
A black bear cub has a sweet tooth.

00:30:08.200 --> 00:30:12.640
But he's never come across bees before and he doesn't know that they will defend their

00:30:12.640 --> 00:30:19.640
honey to the death.

00:30:19.640 --> 00:30:24.720
Stinging is the ultimate self-sacrifice for the bee because it can't pull out its barbed

00:30:24.720 --> 00:30:28.880
sting.

00:30:28.880 --> 00:30:43.800
So its body is torn apart.

00:30:43.800 --> 00:30:48.800
The bees attack was far too much for the cub.

00:30:48.800 --> 00:30:55.280
But a greater challenge is coming.

00:30:55.280 --> 00:31:00.040
The cub's mother.

00:31:00.040 --> 00:31:18.560
The bees fight so hard that she only has time to steal a single comb.

00:31:18.560 --> 00:31:32.320
While the family enjoy their honey at a safe distance, the bees appear to have been defeated.

00:31:32.320 --> 00:31:37.720
Their home has been destroyed and their young will inevitably die out in the open.

00:31:37.720 --> 00:31:43.520
Yet the colony is not doomed because the workers are now rescuing at least some of what really

00:31:43.520 --> 00:31:44.520
matters.

00:31:44.520 --> 00:31:50.360
Their honey.

00:31:50.360 --> 00:32:02.480
Each survivor is able to eat its own weight in honey, storing it in its stomach.

00:32:02.480 --> 00:32:06.600
They will have to abandon their dying young, but they will take much of their precious

00:32:06.600 --> 00:32:14.960
honey with them to sustain themselves while they build a new colony.

00:32:14.960 --> 00:32:22.640
Working together in such an organized society is the insect's great innovation.

00:32:22.640 --> 00:32:32.120
So how did it begin?

00:32:32.120 --> 00:32:42.480
It may have started in a place like this, the nest of the Japanese red bug.

00:32:42.480 --> 00:32:54.080
Parent insects don't usually care for their offspring, but this bug is different.

00:32:54.080 --> 00:33:05.200
The young feed on fallen fruit from just one kind of rare tree.

00:33:05.200 --> 00:33:26.180
They could never find it by themselves, so their mother collects it for them.

00:33:26.180 --> 00:33:37.440
She probes every fruit she finds to test its ripeness and rejects one after another.

00:33:37.440 --> 00:33:43.120
This can take hours.

00:33:43.120 --> 00:33:50.000
At last a perfect fruit.

00:33:50.000 --> 00:34:01.680
A thief, it's easier to steal than find fruit for yourself.

00:34:01.680 --> 00:34:10.440
For both bugs, the outcome of this dispute will be life-changing.

00:34:10.440 --> 00:34:23.680
As the mother struggles to keep her prize, her young back in the nest are growing restless.

00:34:23.680 --> 00:34:26.040
The thief has won.

00:34:26.040 --> 00:34:31.080
The mother doesn't know that her hungry young are leaving their nest in search of a better

00:34:31.080 --> 00:34:40.680
provider.

00:34:40.680 --> 00:34:56.920
She returns to find that her nest is empty.

00:34:56.920 --> 00:35:04.800
Her young find the nest of the thief, who is herself a mother.

00:35:04.800 --> 00:35:16.080
She returns to find that she has twice as many mouths to feed.

00:35:16.080 --> 00:35:26.280
The loser has failed at her only chance of parenthood.

00:35:26.280 --> 00:35:30.480
For the thief, life has suddenly gotten much harder.

00:35:30.480 --> 00:35:34.600
A fruit won't now last as long as it did.

00:35:34.600 --> 00:35:58.240
The young clamber over her, demanding more.

00:35:58.240 --> 00:36:06.680
She returns with another fruit, but her enlarged and insatiable brood wants still more.

00:36:06.680 --> 00:36:25.120
So she has to work non-stop, day after day.

00:36:25.120 --> 00:36:30.640
At last, the young become big enough to fend for themselves, but their success has come

00:36:30.640 --> 00:36:33.080
at a cost.

00:36:33.080 --> 00:36:37.320
The mother has worked herself to death.

00:36:37.320 --> 00:36:45.320
And now her body has become their last meal before they leave home.

00:36:45.320 --> 00:36:53.360
Looking after the young in such a way is thought to be the first step towards living in a community.

00:36:53.360 --> 00:37:03.320
But some insects benefit from doing the opposite of collaborating.

00:37:03.320 --> 00:37:07.680
They kill one another.

00:37:07.680 --> 00:37:13.640
Dawson's bee is one of the largest in the world.

00:37:13.640 --> 00:37:18.240
It nests in the baked soil of the Australian outback.

00:37:18.240 --> 00:37:31.880
Bees are rare in this desert, so colonies of these bees are few and far between.

00:37:31.880 --> 00:37:45.240
The bees excavate tunnels for their young.

00:37:45.240 --> 00:37:50.320
The community is so harmonious that it's hard to believe that this place is also the

00:37:50.320 --> 00:37:56.840
setting for mass murder.

00:37:56.840 --> 00:38:02.200
Tempers do flare when a bee gets confused and goes down another's burrow, but these

00:38:02.200 --> 00:38:08.840
are minor disagreements.

00:38:08.840 --> 00:38:15.060
The colony is peaceful at the moment because every bee here is a female.

00:38:15.060 --> 00:38:23.680
It's the males that are the killers, and they are all dead.

00:38:23.680 --> 00:38:32.560
Their story began two months earlier, before the appearance of the females.

00:38:32.560 --> 00:38:41.700
Male Dawson's bees.

00:38:41.700 --> 00:38:47.120
The females are only just emerging, burrowing their way one by one out of the tunnels where

00:38:47.120 --> 00:38:54.200
they hatched and grew up, and their scent inflames the males.

00:38:54.200 --> 00:39:02.160
They are huge and built for fighting, and each one wants to be in the best position

00:39:02.160 --> 00:39:20.160
to mate with a female.

00:39:20.160 --> 00:39:28.260
A female emerges, and immediately there's a brawl as every male tries to reach her.

00:39:28.260 --> 00:39:33.360
It's very rare for animals to kill their own kind in combat, but stinging and biting rivals

00:39:33.360 --> 00:39:50.320
to death is usually the only way these male bees get a female.

00:39:50.320 --> 00:39:55.440
A winner manages to claim a female, and the pair race for cover.

00:39:55.440 --> 00:40:06.580
Meanwhile, the females' lingering scent drives one unsuccessful male into a deadly frenzy.

00:40:06.580 --> 00:40:19.160
Another female caught in the middle of another brawl becomes an accidental casualty.

00:40:19.160 --> 00:40:24.260
By the time the last female has emerged, every male is dead.

00:40:24.260 --> 00:40:34.120
Battles have ensured that the strongest males will have mated with the most females.

00:40:34.120 --> 00:40:40.920
Two months later, and it's an all-female world with the next generation already developing

00:40:40.920 --> 00:40:47.920
underground.

00:40:47.920 --> 00:41:10.160
Insects have evolved sophisticated societies that, in complexity, are the closest thing

00:41:10.160 --> 00:41:24.960
in nature to our own cities.

00:41:24.960 --> 00:41:31.440
The insects that built this structure in Argentina dominate the surrounding grasslands thanks

00:41:31.440 --> 00:41:41.040
to skills that seem almost more human than insect.

00:41:41.040 --> 00:41:48.840
It's a metropolis, a home to millions of ants.

00:41:48.840 --> 00:41:53.320
The colony needs a huge supply of food.

00:41:53.320 --> 00:42:01.440
It's surrounded by grass, but the ants themselves can't digest a blade of it.

00:42:01.440 --> 00:42:06.400
Nonetheless, they collect it.

00:42:06.400 --> 00:42:18.180
Although they're all the same species, they exist in different shapes and sizes.

00:42:18.180 --> 00:42:24.400
The heads of these big-jawed individuals are not full of brain, but brawn.

00:42:24.400 --> 00:42:26.680
They give the species its name.

00:42:26.680 --> 00:42:38.140
They are grass cutters.

00:42:38.140 --> 00:42:57.080
They work all day, every day.

00:42:57.080 --> 00:43:14.800
The small ants down on the ground are porters with the job of carrying the grass segments

00:43:14.800 --> 00:43:28.120
back to the colony.

00:43:28.120 --> 00:43:31.080
They have great strength.

00:43:31.080 --> 00:43:46.520
Their main problem is balance.

00:43:46.520 --> 00:44:14.480
Columns of them carry the grass back to the nest along highways as straight as Roman roads.

00:44:14.480 --> 00:44:22.680
Ant members of the colony specialize in particular tasks in much the same way as people do.

00:44:22.680 --> 00:44:29.040
But, as in human society, there can be problems.

00:44:29.040 --> 00:44:34.680
A hitchhiker makes one ant's job much harder.

00:44:34.680 --> 00:44:59.080
This isn't even grass.

00:44:59.080 --> 00:45:05.360
The single colony harvests half a ton of grass a year, more than any other animal on these

00:45:05.360 --> 00:45:06.800
plains.

00:45:06.800 --> 00:45:17.120
But since they themselves can't eat it, why do they do so?

00:45:17.120 --> 00:45:19.960
The answer lies underground.

00:45:19.960 --> 00:45:27.720
They have one of the most extraordinary survival strategies in the insect world.

00:45:27.720 --> 00:45:38.820
The ants have dug a network of tunnels that extend downwards for over seven meters.

00:45:38.820 --> 00:45:42.800
At the heart of the colony lies the key to their survival.

00:45:42.800 --> 00:45:44.160
A fungus.

00:45:44.160 --> 00:45:46.520
But this isn't sloppy housekeeping.

00:45:46.520 --> 00:45:54.600
This is a fungus that is found nowhere else on earth.

00:45:54.600 --> 00:45:58.040
The ants cultivate it assiduously.

00:45:58.040 --> 00:46:04.520
The big jawed ants chop up the grass, covering it with an antibiotic saliva that kills every

00:46:04.520 --> 00:46:11.040
kind of fungus, except this one.

00:46:11.040 --> 00:46:14.400
The ants are farmers.

00:46:14.400 --> 00:46:23.280
They feed the grass to the fungus, and the fungus thrives.

00:46:23.280 --> 00:46:29.760
The ants cultivate dozens of these fungus gardens throughout the colony.

00:46:29.760 --> 00:46:33.080
This is what they eat.

00:46:33.080 --> 00:46:41.280
The system is so efficient that a single colony can have five million members.

00:46:41.280 --> 00:46:46.840
But the fungus is also dangerous.

00:46:46.840 --> 00:46:53.840
As it grows, it releases carbon dioxide that could asphyxiate the entire colony.

00:46:53.840 --> 00:46:59.840
The ants' way of dealing with this danger is ingenious.

00:46:59.840 --> 00:47:07.000
They construct their nest so that it has an automatic air conditioning system.

00:47:07.000 --> 00:47:13.080
The outer surface is so shaped that the slightest breeze sucks out stale air through these central

00:47:13.080 --> 00:47:14.080
vents.

00:47:14.080 --> 00:47:20.520
At the same time, fresh air is drawn down through the outer vents right into the heart

00:47:20.520 --> 00:47:28.160
of the nest.

00:47:28.160 --> 00:47:34.600
Our own societies have existed for thousands of years, but insect societies have lasted

00:47:34.600 --> 00:47:35.600
for millions.

00:47:35.600 --> 00:47:52.480
There may be 10 million different kinds of insects, and there are 200 million individuals

00:47:52.480 --> 00:47:57.200
for every one of us.

00:47:57.200 --> 00:48:03.240
The insects' flexible armor and their adaptability has made them the most abundant and the most

00:48:03.240 --> 00:48:30.000
diverse animal group in our planet's history.

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:37.120
The Sierra Madre Mountains in Mexico are home to one of the greatest natural wonders.

00:48:37.120 --> 00:48:49.280
A billion bright orange butterflies offered the life team a wonderful filming challenge.

00:48:49.280 --> 00:49:04.440
The colonies are only found above 3,000 meters, and the team trek there slowly in the thin

00:49:04.440 --> 00:49:09.520
air.

00:49:09.520 --> 00:49:14.720
The monarch phenomenon was only discovered here around 30 years ago, and one man has

00:49:14.720 --> 00:49:19.520
been studying them every year since, Professor Lincoln Brower.

00:49:19.520 --> 00:49:24.680
His knowledge will be essential if the team are to achieve their goal of flying a camera

00:49:24.680 --> 00:49:26.880
among the butterflies.

00:49:26.880 --> 00:49:33.160
It's amazing that the butterflies only come to this area that's about 30 by 60 miles in

00:49:33.160 --> 00:49:35.000
extent.

00:49:35.000 --> 00:49:41.280
There are other areas that have high forests in different parts of Mexico, to the west

00:49:41.280 --> 00:49:46.640
of here, to the south of here, but as far as we know, this is the only place that monarchs

00:49:46.640 --> 00:49:50.000
are able to spend the winter.

00:49:50.000 --> 00:49:54.840
The butterflies are in such numbers that filming them the traditional way from a camera on

00:49:54.840 --> 00:49:58.620
a tripod is reasonably straightforward.

00:49:58.620 --> 00:50:04.520
But to get a butterfly's viewpoint by flying a camera amongst the swarms is going to be

00:50:04.520 --> 00:50:09.760
altogether more difficult.

00:50:09.760 --> 00:50:17.640
First the team need a spot where butterflies gather in huge numbers.

00:50:17.640 --> 00:50:24.680
Luckily Lincoln knows a place where thousands come down to drink every day.

00:50:24.680 --> 00:50:28.480
The crew spend the next three days preparing the equipment.

00:50:28.480 --> 00:50:34.200
A remote controlled camera, a lot of cable, two bicycle wheels, a bag of rocks, and some

00:50:34.200 --> 00:50:39.000
muscle power.

00:50:39.000 --> 00:50:50.520
Now all they need to do is wait until the butterfly numbers reach truly impressive proportions.

00:50:50.520 --> 00:50:55.760
By midday the butterflies are swarming and it's time for the camera to glide with them

00:50:55.760 --> 00:50:56.760
through the air.

00:50:56.760 --> 00:50:57.760
Three and away.

00:50:57.760 --> 00:51:12.240
That's it, stunning.

00:51:12.240 --> 00:51:20.000
We've got great numbers coming in, so let's just keep going and then we'll do a turn round

00:51:20.000 --> 00:51:21.000
shot.

00:51:21.000 --> 00:51:29.040
A series of unique shots flying with the butterflies.

00:51:29.040 --> 00:51:36.760
But that was the easy part.

00:51:36.760 --> 00:51:43.640
Now it's time to get the camera 50 metres up into the tree tops.

00:51:43.640 --> 00:51:46.600
Filming the roosting butterflies is not going to be simple.

00:51:46.600 --> 00:51:50.680
The clusters are unstable.

00:51:50.680 --> 00:51:56.400
And they can suddenly disperse without warning what Lincoln calls an explosion.

00:51:56.400 --> 00:51:59.280
Here's another explosion, wow look at that.

00:51:59.280 --> 00:52:03.560
And they're going right over our head.

00:52:03.560 --> 00:52:09.640
Spectacular no doubt, but a real problem for the climbing team, Jim Spickler and Tim Fogg.

00:52:09.640 --> 00:52:11.960
Would it be better for us to start now?

00:52:11.960 --> 00:52:15.480
We're going to be very careful not to disturb them or limit our disturbance.

00:52:15.480 --> 00:52:20.280
But if we do disturb them, right now it seems that they're warm enough that they could relocate

00:52:20.280 --> 00:52:22.840
to a cluster right adjacent to them.

00:52:22.840 --> 00:52:24.840
Do you think that's true?

00:52:24.840 --> 00:52:30.520
If you disturb them they tend to fly away from the tree 50 feet or so.

00:52:30.520 --> 00:52:34.400
So you'll lose those clusters I think if you disturb them.

00:52:34.400 --> 00:52:39.160
Do you think they'll cluster again tomorrow night?

00:52:39.160 --> 00:52:40.360
I doubt it.

00:52:40.360 --> 00:52:44.080
I think they'll just move down.

00:52:44.080 --> 00:52:46.400
That's really hard stuff.

00:52:46.400 --> 00:52:52.400
Because I can't see how we can get up the trees and do what we have to up there, close

00:52:52.400 --> 00:52:54.680
to any clusters without them flying.

00:52:54.680 --> 00:52:58.080
I just don't know how I'm going to be able to get an arrow in the tree.

00:52:58.080 --> 00:53:03.360
If I could have you picked your tree you could put the arrow in it now.

00:53:03.360 --> 00:53:08.960
The prospect of rigging cables high in the canopy without disturbing the butterflies

00:53:08.960 --> 00:53:10.680
is a daunting one.

00:53:10.680 --> 00:53:13.600
They may only get one chance.

00:53:13.600 --> 00:53:15.240
One false move and it'll all be gone.

00:53:15.240 --> 00:53:18.880
And I sort of had the impression that they might come back again but Lincoln says they

00:53:18.880 --> 00:53:19.880
just won't.

00:53:19.880 --> 00:53:21.280
They'll just move on to another tree.

00:53:21.280 --> 00:53:27.400
So we'll put all the rigging in, disturb them and that's it.

00:53:27.400 --> 00:53:31.160
Choosing which trees to rig is a critical decision.

00:53:31.160 --> 00:53:43.400
Can you get right to the tree number six tree then?

00:53:43.400 --> 00:53:51.520
They begin by firing a line over a suitably high branch.

00:53:51.520 --> 00:54:02.840
That'll work.

00:54:02.840 --> 00:54:12.640
Nobody, not even Lincoln, knows how the butterflies will react.

00:54:12.640 --> 00:54:14.880
Yeah that's going to work perfect I think.

00:54:14.880 --> 00:54:15.880
Okay great.

00:54:15.880 --> 00:54:26.880
I've got to do a change over here so just give me a moment.

00:54:26.880 --> 00:54:34.560
There's an explosion.

00:54:34.560 --> 00:54:49.600
Thankfully it's not in the trees that Jim and Tim are rigging with such care.

00:54:49.600 --> 00:54:52.080
Can you just push it towards me a bit in fact?

00:54:52.080 --> 00:54:53.080
Oh ow.

00:54:53.080 --> 00:54:54.080
You all right okay?

00:54:54.080 --> 00:54:55.080
Yeah.

00:54:55.080 --> 00:54:56.080
Okay get your fingers out.

00:54:56.080 --> 00:55:00.040
Okay that's good.

00:55:00.040 --> 00:55:05.480
It takes another three days to get the aerial rig set up followed by many hours of fine

00:55:05.480 --> 00:55:06.480
tuning.

00:55:06.480 --> 00:55:16.080
Just pan it so we don't see it in the monitor please.

00:55:16.080 --> 00:55:21.480
Everything is finally set and the butterflies are still there.

00:55:21.480 --> 00:55:28.760
One two three.

00:55:28.760 --> 00:55:37.760
Take one.

00:55:37.760 --> 00:55:38.760
Too slow.

00:55:38.760 --> 00:55:48.560
There was no load at the beginning so suddenly it just completely stopped.

00:55:48.560 --> 00:55:51.560
Take six.

00:55:51.560 --> 00:55:54.560
Start to slow now.

00:55:54.560 --> 00:55:55.560
Keep going.

00:55:55.560 --> 00:55:56.560
Start to slow.

00:55:56.560 --> 00:55:57.560
Keep going.

00:55:57.560 --> 00:55:58.560
Okay Mike hold it.

00:55:58.560 --> 00:55:59.560
We've got enough.

00:55:59.560 --> 00:56:00.560
We've not got enough weight Jim.

00:56:00.560 --> 00:56:01.560
Take nine.

00:56:01.560 --> 00:56:02.560
Go on Mike.

00:56:02.560 --> 00:56:03.560
Go on Mike.

00:56:03.560 --> 00:56:04.560
Getting close Mike.

00:56:04.560 --> 00:56:05.560
Now start slowing.

00:56:05.560 --> 00:56:06.560
Okay so.

00:56:06.560 --> 00:56:23.440
It's still not right and now time is running out.

00:56:23.440 --> 00:56:24.800
I think the light's going.

00:56:24.800 --> 00:56:25.800
Can you slow?

00:56:25.800 --> 00:56:27.480
Can you do one last take?

00:56:27.480 --> 00:56:32.440
All right because this one's here they're losing the light on them.

00:56:32.440 --> 00:56:36.960
Try and get a shot that's going fast here if it's smooth.

00:56:36.960 --> 00:56:41.480
One two three and we're away.

00:56:41.480 --> 00:56:49.040
Take 17.

00:56:49.040 --> 00:56:50.440
And it's looking good.

00:56:50.440 --> 00:56:54.560
Okay start slowing Mike.

00:56:54.560 --> 00:56:59.680
Okay keep it going keep it going.

00:56:59.680 --> 00:57:05.720
Success at last.

00:57:05.720 --> 00:57:12.240
With the technique now working the crew rig more and more shots to give an aerial perspective

00:57:12.240 --> 00:57:20.800
on the monarch phenomenon.

00:57:20.800 --> 00:57:26.400
With his work advising the crew over Lincoln continues the observations he's been making

00:57:26.400 --> 00:57:28.920
for over a quarter of a century.

00:57:28.920 --> 00:57:33.500
Although the butterflies are still here in immense numbers he's worried there is a threat

00:57:33.500 --> 00:57:35.700
to their survival.

00:57:35.700 --> 00:57:43.080
The big huge influx maybe sometimes as many as two billion butterflies flying down into

00:57:43.080 --> 00:57:51.280
this tiny area of 30 by 60 miles is thoroughly endangered by illegal logging.

00:57:51.280 --> 00:58:01.480
If the forest is not protected the phenomenon will be lost.

00:58:01.480 --> 00:58:08.800
That'd be a disaster.

00:58:08.800 --> 00:58:15.960
I mean it would be like taking the Mona Lisa out of the Parisian Museum and burning it

00:58:15.960 --> 00:58:39.920
up.
